-- title:
--   The coordinate of an adic point avoids K
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $K$, let $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$, and let $C$ be a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, decidable equality and a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$. Let $R$ be a commutative $\mathcal{O}$-algebra with a map to $C$, compatibly with the maps from $\mathcal{O}$ and $K$ to $C$. Let $\varpi$ be a pseudo-uniformizer: an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K$ whose valuation in $C$ lies strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N \in \mathbb{N}$. Assume `IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image exactly the valuation ring $\{v \le 1\}$ of $C$, $R$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$, the elements of $K$ of valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal{O}$, the image of $K$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi.\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Then for every adic point $x$ of $\Omega$ over $R$ — a system of Deligne data over $R/\pi^{n+1}$ for all $n$, compatible under the transition maps — the coordinate $x.toOmega\,C$, defined as the unique $z \in C$ with $(z,1)$ in the $C$-span of the image in $C^2$ of the submodule `x.stdLine` of $R^2$ (and as $0$ if no such unique $z$ exists), lies in `Omega.upperHalfPlane K C`, the complement in $C$ of the image of $K$. In particular, since $0$ does lie in that image, the conclusion also forces the unique-existence condition defining $x.toOmega$ to hold.
--
--   This is the statement that the canonical coordinate of an adic point of the formal upper half plane lands in Drinfeld's $p$-adic upper half plane $\Omega = C \setminus K$, the irrationality being forced by the nondegeneracy (edge) condition in the Deligne datum at the prime of $R/\pi$. It is used to identify the unit locus of an edge chart in terms of the coordinate, and in the covering and linear-pieces statements for the Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient.
